he curve we have $x=4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ &=(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})i+(- 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(- 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{16\\sin^2t+16\\cos^2t} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{16(\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t)} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{16} \\\\ &=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=- y^{2}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(-1)(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2 \\\\ &=-16\\sin^2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C - y^{2}\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0}(- 16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)})\\cdot (4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0}- 64 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=-64\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0}\\sin^2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=-64\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0}\\frac{1-\\cos 2t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=-32\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0}1-\\cos 2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=(-32)\\left[t-\\frac{\\sin 2t}{2}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0} \\\\ &=\\left[(- 32 t)+(16 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)})\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{0} \\\\ &=\\left((- 32 (0))+(16 \\sin{\\left(2 (0) \\right)})\\right)-\\left((- 32 (- \\frac{\\pi}{4}))+(16 \\sin{\\left(2 (- \\frac{\\pi}{4}) \\right)})\\right) \\\\ &=\\left((0)+(0)\\right)-\\left((8 \\pi)+(-16)\\right) \\\\ &=16 - 8 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520725 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "432eecfb-20a2-5095-b3ec-3e76f9097706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 216 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520674} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf41ec2-f489-5de9-a19a-a47e5625943d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 6 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520679} {:answer/id #uuid "6b48cc25-c525-5393-8d7c-54a84b821d56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520687} {:answer/id #uuid "82514b89-cc6b-5812-8d65-61e3a30d237a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520694} {:answer/id #uuid "9d4da34d-0d0a-5dea-b628-7489bccb6cc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cf41ec2-f489-5de9-a19a-a47e5625943d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 6 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520679} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf9a4aeb-294c-5c91-aea0-4c28b6189145" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e949b5e8-0b4c-52ac-b46c-4d2d9167f454" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=- \\frac{3}{- x + y}$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨3 t,\\,3 t + 6⟩$ for $t\\in [-2,2].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=⟨3 t,\\,3 t + 6⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=3 t$ and $y=3 t + 6.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 t + 6)j \\\\ &=(3)i+(3)j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(3)^2+(3)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{18} \\\\ &=3 \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=- \\frac{3}{- x + y}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=\\frac{-3}{(3 t + 6)-(3 t)} \\\\ &=\\frac{-3}{6} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C - \\frac{3}{- x + y}\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{-2}^{2}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-2}^{2}(- \\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (3 \\sqrt{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=(3 \\sqrt{2})\\int_{-2}^{2}- \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-2}^{2}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2}}{2})\\left[t\\right]_{-2}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left[(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2} t}{2})\\right]_{-2}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left((- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2} (2)}{2})\\right)-\\left((- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{2} (-2)}{2})\\right) \\\\ &=\\left((- 3 \\sqrt{2})\\right)-\\left((3 \\sqrt{2})\\right) \\\\ &=- 6 \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520728 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "145424bf-f6bb-5942-9d09-e285f663ac59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8 \\pi^{3} \\left(5 + 6 \\pi^{2}\\right)}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520662} {:answer/id #uuid "18d47090-2dd1-5291-bb5a-e6fbf0892724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\pi + \\frac{4 \\pi^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520664} {:answer/id #uuid "be74739c-3ea8-5708-9646-40ef9c2001d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\pi^{2} \\left(2 + \\pi^{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520709} {:answer/id #uuid "bed90742-ab65-5adc-a430-b09e0b81b132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\pi^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520710} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb49685-bf7a-57be-992d-18ab3d8cc6d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\pi^{2} \\left(1 + \\pi^{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebb49685-bf7a-57be-992d-18ab3d8cc6d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\pi^{2} \\left(1 + \\pi^{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520726} :answer-field/id #uuid "289dda4e-7c4b-52ce-ba32-751594b39498" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff8f8180-bdc0-5b6a-a9a7-ec6192daea60" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x^{2} + y^{2} - 2$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $r(t)=⟨2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=⟨2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ &=(2)(-\\sin t+\\sin t+t\\cos t)i+(2)(\\cos t-\\cos t+t\\sin t)j \\\\ &=(2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})i+(2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{4t^2(\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t)} \\\\ &=2|t| \\\\ &=2 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ since $t\\geq0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x^{2} + y^{2} - 2$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(1)\\left((2 t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(- 2 t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2\\right)+(-2) \\\\ &=(4)\\left(\\cos^2t+2t\\sin t\\cos t+t^2\\sin^2t+\\sin^2t-2t\\sin t\\cos t+t^2\\cos^2t\\right)+(-2) \\\\ &=(4)(1+t^2)+(-2) \\\\ &=4 t^{2} + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x^{2} + y^{2} - 2\\,\\text{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}(4 t^{2} + 2)\\cdot (2 t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}8 t^{3} + 4 t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[(2 t^{2})+(2 t^{4})\\right]_{0}^{\\pi} \\\\ &=\\left((2 (\\pi)^{2})+(2 (\\pi)^{4})\\right)-\\left((2 (0)^{2})+(2 (0)^{4})\\right) \\\\ &=\\left((2 \\pi^{2})+(2 \\pi^{4})\\right)-\\left((0)+(0)\\right) \\\\ &=2 \\pi^{2} \\left(1 + \\pi^{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "198e90de-3051-575c-bc22-01521b0d1075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{C{(x-6)}^{1/2}-2}{4{(x-6)}^{1/2}})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520730} {:answer/id #uuid "347f99a3-0ba6-5adf-a322-65df926c05e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4{(x-6)}^{5/4}+C}{3{(x-6)}^{1/4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520731} {:answer/id #uuid "36de1601-f8d7-5c58-8370-c2c78395ace6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{{(x-6)}^{9/4}+C}{9{(x-6)}^{1/4}})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520732} {:answer/id #uuid "830ad737-81e3-515d-810c-86375e9cc662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{{(x-6)}^{5/4}+C}{5{(x-6)}^{1/4}})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520735} {:answer/id #uuid "8d613153-ad03-5279-a752-55d311e9f54d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{(x-6)}^{1/4}+C}{4{(x-6)}^{9/4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36de1601-f8d7-5c58-8370-c2c78395ace6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{{(x-6)}^{9/4}+C}{9{(x-6)}^{1/4}})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520732} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e561d6b-d4ae-5cce-a550-57e45ec5f9c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4c866423-3d16-5bfb-95b6-2aad67327bbd" :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n(x-6)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y={(x-6)}^{2}{y}^{3/4}.\n$$\n\nWork on an interval where $x-6>0$. Choose constants and intervals on which all displayed real expressions are defined. Take $y>0$.\n\n{{selection}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-6)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y={(x-6)}^{2}{y}^{3/4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{(x-6)} & =(x-6){y}^{3/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)={(x-6)}^{-1},\\,q(x)=(x-6),\\,n=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3/4},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3/4}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{(x-6)}^{-1}{y}^{1/4}=(x-6).\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{1/4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{-3/4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-3/4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{(x-6)}^{-1}z=(x-6)\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{4}{(x-6)}^{-1}z=\\frac{(x-6)}{4}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (x-6)},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{(x-6)}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (x-6)})\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{(1/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-6)} \\\\  & ={(x-6)}^{1/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={(x-6)}^{1/4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-6)}^{1/4}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{4}{(x-6)}^{-1}z) & ={(x-6)}^{1/4}\\cdot (\\frac{(x-6)}{4}) \\\\ {(x-6)}^{1/4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{4}{(x-6)}^{-3/4}z & =\\frac{{(x-6)}^{5/4}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{(x-6)}^{1/4}) & =\\frac{{(x-6)}^{5/4}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{(x-6)}^{1/4})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{(x-6)}^{5/4}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{(x-6)}^{1/4} & =\\frac{1}{9}{(x-6)}^{9/4}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{{(x-6)}^{9/4}+C}{9{(x-6)}^{1/4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=9{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{1/4}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{1/4}=\\frac{{(x-6)}^{9/4}+C}{9{(x-6)}^{1/4}}\\,⇒\\,y={(\\frac{{(x-6)}^{9/4}+C}{9{(x-6)}^{1/4}})}^{4}\\,.\n$$\n\nThese are the nonzero real solutions on the specified intervals; exclude denominator zeros and restrict constants and intervals to the real branches used above. Since $z=y^{1/4}>0$, retain only intervals where the displayed expression for $z$ is positive."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521453 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a6e61cd-d186-5792-84d0-8d7d67600c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{68}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520780} {:answer/id #uuid "2a8bc0df-5aa9-5ce3-bcbf-a8928812b638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{66}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520854} {:answer/id #uuid "9bfcee41-0fad-566f-9af6-d545060b165d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{71}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521117} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e2bbae-35c1-5d0c-a925-bfeae261bad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{59}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521205} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e3e47c-bd55-5a69-9a47-9986d09220c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{63}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a6e61cd-d186-5792-84d0-8d7d67600c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{68}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520780} :answer-field/id #uuid "86a8665b-bde3-52c7-b1d3-5fddd8560e4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07caf841-c23b-5d01-856b-51dc74025403" :question/math-academy-id "q-76576" :question/problem "Using a calculator, find the value of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})$ in degrees to the nearest degree." :question/worked-solution "Using a calculator, we find \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) & =(41.810…)^{∘}+(26.565…)^{∘} \\\\  & =(68.375…)^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{68}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest degree."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c44563a-1326-5d99-9881-696a2bb3906d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520784} {:answer/id #uuid "2186a87e-24dc-58c2-a7ab-6583096c289d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520833} {:answer/id #uuid "5eab803d-e2e7-5163-bf15-0810f1431535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520976} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4f4407-5a99-5b48-917e-de381c7c9cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521180} {:answer/id #uuid "ec960d32-a56e-5be1-a636-fdd4c8d72cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5eab803d-e2e7-5163-bf15-0810f1431535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520976} :answer-field/id #uuid "48f0d76a-3c1e-522a-8915-c0902dc2770d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08e98843-e2bc-5a10-8ff2-9ed917780859" :question/math-academy-id "q-24805" :question/problem "![](images/19/197557b866b4a4a1c2c151c29cc529e12a5048b941223fe8e2eb4a7c4959223e.png)\n\nThe diagonals of a square increase in length at a rate of $1.5\\text{ mm/min}.$ At what rate is the area of the square changing when the diagonals are $4\\,\\text{mm}$ long?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is increasing at a rate of $1.5\\,\\text{mm/min},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=1.5.\n$$\n\nAlso, when the diagonals are $4\\,\\text{mm},$ we have $h=4.$ Substituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=1.5\\cdot 4=6{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $6\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521456 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aa873a6-fa87-5112-b328-59b790bb02cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521114} {:answer/id #uuid "a28cf962-96ea-5fd2-a648-e08c58efd301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521129} {:answer/id #uuid "a6084d40-a60b-5595-86d6-684fff85cf4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521135} {:answer/id #uuid "a75bdbec-56f0-53b5-95b6-f2d3c36945c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521139} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3d86cc-339a-5c68-b539-6749321e41e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6084d40-a60b-5595-86d6-684fff85cf4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521135} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8c5f3c7-fd49-5b7c-9172-09702c7f16cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ac5cd87-de15-5914-9f66-8d76ea6b7dee" :question/math-academy-id "q-2555" :question/problem "![](images/89/8946fc7f3b9a087304d893d69196f8462913974aab552b05a442a7460a619bef.png)\n\nWater leaking onto a floor forms a circular pool. The radius of the pool increases at a rate of $6\\,\\text{cm/min}.$ How fast is the area of the pool increasing at the moment when the radius is $\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}?$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=\\frac{2}{\\pi },$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=6.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=6$ and $r=\\frac{2}{\\pi },$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =6\\cdot 2\\pi (\\frac{2}{\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the pool is increasing at a rate of $24{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "468992e9-ad98-5e6d-b726-dd3130a61cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520921} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd828f8-42ce-5777-935d-cf7d0ddd6df5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521057} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6a8bed-d21d-52be-9870-71f5031347b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521116} {:answer/id #uuid "c85bb9fd-5526-511a-8a6a-c2514cf0251b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521212} {:answer/id #uuid "d5002f69-b154-569c-9f75-ac522a011347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c85bb9fd-5526-511a-8a6a-c2514cf0251b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521212} :answer-field/id #uuid "b57e8dee-3a1b-5eb0-a0a9-8388e98ff9a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "20662b40-9142-5228-b622-edf8a16af72c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140476" :question/problem "Rewrite $-8{z}^{2}-12z-\\frac{7}{2}$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-8$. So, we take out a factor of $-8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-8({z}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}z+\\frac{7}{16})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}z+\\frac{7}{16}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot z+\\frac{7}{16}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $\\frac{7}{16}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot z+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{16}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{16}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8({z}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}z+\\frac{7}{16}) & =-8({(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-8{(z+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521473 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15aac7dc-8cd1-5607-ba90-095a24d98488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520815} {:answer/id #uuid "35f3791a-7cf7-5558-b05a-1693353bfa6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520879} {:answer/id #uuid "3b71401b-cf88-5840-8c19-a4e492d787c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520895} {:answer/id #uuid "a8691977-29a4-53b2-bc0b-9e23c70a02aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521142} {:answer/id #uuid "ce511b28-cdc4-5fcb-a0b8-330fbc0beae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b71401b-cf88-5840-8c19-a4e492d787c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520895} :answer-field/id #uuid "bffaf27a-e722-5e41-898d-3903f71f347b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "299f3633-d5e0-546a-8fb6-eacb46c1b2dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-123549" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{20}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{9})$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{50}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{18})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{80}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{9})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${20}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{20}^{∘}=20\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Converting ${50}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{50}^{∘}=50\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{5\\pi }{18}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Converting ${80}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{80}^{∘}=80\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{4\\pi }{9}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{4\\pi }{9}\\ne \\frac{2\\pi }{9}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I and II only'."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f798b7f-7d7a-5343-9738-1b34c019d8a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f798b7f-7d7a-5343-9738-1b34c019d8a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521089} :answer-field/id #uuid "3836a26d-16ce-5b20-91e1-5b41083d18ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "37d3dfcf-127d-51c3-8809-c6a386eec680" :question/math-academy-id "q-314510" :question/problem "![](images/00/007ac521cd9bd97773aa18e243e788ce9eb473a65e3242022539266bc8f23ef4.png)\n\nA laser technician is reducing the diagonals of a square aperture at a rate of $2.4\\text{ mm}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the aperture decreasing when the diagonals are $12.5\\,\\text{mm}$ long each?\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe area is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ decreases at a rate of $2.4\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=12.5.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.4\\cdot 12.5=-30{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square aperture is decreasing at a rate of $30{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24e8e760-7f22-552c-92c8-7d281f94ccea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520842} {:answer/id #uuid "2d802dae-c64e-582a-850a-c8d967eab5f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520860} {:answer/id #uuid "3917eb06-1a11-50d1-a6bb-6d7d1b2d7a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520888} {:answer/id #uuid "3f23a7ae-da79-5087-902a-7b787f048816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520906} {:answer/id #uuid "4d275836-192b-5d91-b4e5-fe1fa63b7154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3917eb06-1a11-50d1-a6bb-6d7d1b2d7a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520888} :answer-field/id #uuid "76bea3b5-fc60-57ec-bd8b-23889bb99a1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4cd34dd2-403c-55c2-9000-3c54447738a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-123545" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{57}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{19\\pi }{60})$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{32}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{8\\pi }{35})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{14}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{80})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${57}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{57}^{∘}=57\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{19\\pi }{60}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Converting ${32}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{32}^{∘}=32\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{8\\pi }{45}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{8\\pi }{45}\\ne \\frac{8\\pi }{35}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Converting ${14}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{14}^{∘}=14\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{7\\pi }{90}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{7\\pi }{90}\\ne \\frac{7\\pi }{80}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I only'."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521498 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20b415d0-ce3e-5252-babf-70efd7ce0657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520831} {:answer/id #uuid "58c31464-2ecd-5861-b8f8-f20ce9749e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520966} {:answer/id #uuid "78a7f04a-4b10-5b42-9c84-6a290d024e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521034} {:answer/id #uuid "c589619a-7081-5009-a45d-0f03134703e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521204} {:answer/id #uuid "d1368367-9652-53bc-8d18-404303318464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58c31464-2ecd-5861-b8f8-f20ce9749e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520966} :answer-field/id #uuid "1af2aa0f-c2a3-543f-a83e-691c13f6818c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5168a992-4557-5020-911a-67c9df9985ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-123537" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{15}^{∘}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{20}^{∘}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{36})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{35}^{∘}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{36})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${15}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{15}^{∘}=15\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Converting ${20}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{20}^{∘}=20\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{36}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Converting ${35}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{35}^{∘}=35\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{7\\pi }{36}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I and III only'."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b772459-363c-5a44-a11a-d79c4d0978f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b772459-363c-5a44-a11a-d79c4d0978f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520856} :answer-field/id #uuid "7afa75a5-ca2e-53b7-86f8-9f195e705814" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53b5eaef-0c6b-5313-b037-03a8dbcbc92d" :question/math-academy-id "q-313985" :question/problem "A spotlight $S$ is on the ground $6\\,\\text{m}$ away from a wall and a $1.5\\,\\text{m}$ tall robot is moving towards the wall at a rate of $0.6\\,\\text{m/s}.$ To **one decimal place**, how fast is the height of the shadow on the wall decreasing when the robot is $2.5\\,\\text{m}$ from the spotlight?\n\nThe shadow decreases at a speed of approximately \n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/e9/e91c4e08aa785de1fe500c2f43e0981501ee597bb7bb042827154842ec06ad0c.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the robot’s distance from the wall, while $y$ is the length of the shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the robot’s velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-0.6\\,\\text{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the robot’s velocity is *negative* because it is moving *toward* the wall, and therefore the distance between it and the wall *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{(6-x)+x} & =\\frac{1.5}{6-x} \\\\ \\frac{y}{6} & =\\frac{1.5}{6-x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{9}{6-x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{(6-x)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=6-2.5=3.5\\,\\text{m}$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-0.6\\,\\text{m/s}$ into the above expression gives, to one decimal place\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{{2.5}^{2}}\\cdot (-0.6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & ≈-0.9\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shadow decreases at a speed of approximately  $0.9\\,\\text{m/s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "445dc765-fffa-5c9c-8032-001531d309ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520917} {:answer/id #uuid "7c75d6e2-200f-56c9-be87-9792e2c68716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521049} {:answer/id #uuid "a1dd6c8f-7899-5358-a311-16926a65bd02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521127} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a7853a-28a8-54cd-8b7e-385d6189ab0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521130} {:answer/id #uuid "b35bbdff-1376-5898-ab4b-cfff11b72c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b35bbdff-1376-5898-ab4b-cfff11b72c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521165} :answer-field/id #uuid "740f5659-04b8-5006-82f4-2605026e1fc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57848bee-3e72-5d15-a97c-92bd970ffedc" :question/math-academy-id "q-99753" :question/problem "What is $0÷(-\\frac{8}{21})?$" :question/worked-solution "Zero divided by any number (other than zero) equals zero. Therefore, \n\n$$\n0÷(-\\frac{8}{21})=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521509 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12483b7c-75aa-5d6b-b9c2-3a38b6a59e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{32.8}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520803} {:answer/id #uuid "351eb816-1272-52d2-90b9-bfc766cd81a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{24.6}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520875} {:answer/id #uuid "5d891148-a3a7-5819-a73c-eeda3eab70c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{27.5}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520974} {:answer/id #uuid "d1176aad-386c-5cb7-8300-8d79fa09268e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30.5}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521233} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b32859-f535-52b8-b810-054604e1f38b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30.2}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d891148-a3a7-5819-a73c-eeda3eab70c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{27.5}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520974} :answer-field/id #uuid "03c2a9e1-24c8-5afe-a713-5a7daad4ef0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63ad50c7-f590-525f-9856-7ab603587d0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-4546" :question/problem "![](images/40/404e9d30d45485251a19cdb2ccfef4ea53509a66437c99908eda065ad99e5e82.png)\n\nRounded to one decimal place, $x≈$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given the lengths of the opposite side and the hypotenuse, we can use sine to find the angle measurement. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{6}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now calculate $x$ using inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{6}{13} \\\\ \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{6}{13}) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{6}{13}) \\\\ x & =(27.486\\,4…)^{∘} \\\\ x & ≈{27.5}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae33c09f-da88-5496-ac0c-693ebc91e98f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae33c09f-da88-5496-ac0c-693ebc91e98f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521152} :answer-field/id #uuid "e74b5676-bbc7-5018-8226-6434bdbb2081" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7842bd80-84fe-5d31-bbd1-38dac51d5756" :question/math-academy-id "q-344787" :question/problem "A spherical hailstone is growing as it moves through a storm cloud. At a particular moment, the rate of increase in the volume of the hailstone is numerically equal to three times the rate of increase in its surface area. What is the radius of the hailstone at that moment? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the hailstone, $V$ the volume, and $A$ its surface area. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the volume $V$ and the surface area $A$ of the sphere. Since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2}$ and $V=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3},$ the relationship between $V$ and $A$ is\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{(\\frac{A}{4\\pi })}^{3/2}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{3(4\\pi )^{1/2}}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}A}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }} \\\\ {A}^{1/2} & =12\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ A & =(12\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2} \\\\ A & =144\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\pi {r}^{2} & =144\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =36 \\\\ r & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12e5b434-b339-517f-8e76-65e3a8e74c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{63}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520805} {:answer/id #uuid "392938b3-fafd-5d04-96a2-6f65b79f2214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{59}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520889} {:answer/id #uuid "5294be99-f335-5f00-9dc1-33495c1ddccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{56}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520951} {:answer/id #uuid "592dd33b-32ce-5762-b91a-04fda1691cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{49}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520968} {:answer/id #uuid "936b2fb6-abb5-5942-9464-f53912a834de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{67}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5294be99-f335-5f00-9dc1-33495c1ddccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{56}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520951} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e7bf571-3da0-5ba9-9c54-e3a915fafd86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d8ea2fc-3731-5b48-8288-39aacc74f9f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-122419" :question/problem "Find the value of  $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})$ in degrees to the nearest degree." :question/worked-solution "Using a calculator, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) & =(56.309…)^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{56}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest degree."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8b64d1a-7608-5a86-ab17-d145feb343f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8b64d1a-7608-5a86-ab17-d145feb343f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521308} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eb427c0-14cf-57c4-87a9-e84597ae69e6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521386} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d75106f5-9234-558c-a391-c36bbf650407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d75106f5-9234-558c-a391-c36bbf650407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521245} :answer-field/id #uuid "90dc3305-1623-5c23-9c54-7ae3dcbc7551" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c5fa084-327e-5d66-96c0-960c479aa7b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-218663" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({z}^{2}+z+2)$ by completing the square.\n\n$-({z}^{2}+z+2)=-(z\\,+$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+z+2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}z+2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}z+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({z}^{2}+z+2) & =-({(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(z+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ffe8b5a-89d1-5cc4-a628-30dfce5ca486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520798} {:answer/id #uuid "1d6158b8-7fa1-5374-8102-b315d5ee7d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520827} {:answer/id #uuid "4c55ebeb-7cce-5cfe-953e-91fd1d9a3ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520938} {:answer/id #uuid "d4266c13-546a-5d43-a39f-e09844714267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521240} {:answer/id #uuid "f3400f05-0efd-579c-9c4d-2bee0c52b9db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c55ebeb-7cce-5cfe-953e-91fd1d9a3ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520938} :answer-field/id #uuid "716b3e70-e198-5303-8455-9f55371b225a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "934b60d8-f889-5c91-8ad2-ede254ef4d64" :question/math-academy-id "q-54418" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians to an equivalent measure in degrees. This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })=\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{4}={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since we know that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521530 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61de9dc8-279b-5f59-904b-21e15c2aa717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61de9dc8-279b-5f59-904b-21e15c2aa717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520986} :answer-field/id #uuid "046001e6-52ad-564f-b292-d2455a9b9819" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9793c42f-eccf-5af0-a22e-1cfcdfee54a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-344674" :question/problem "![](images/34/34a41e39a36d3e073ef2ca4144bd346b340efa22a9da7d3e8a9df48d32d900b3.png)\n\nA square opening in a testing chamber is being reduced, causing the area of the opening to decrease at a rate of $49{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate are the diagonals decreasing when each diagonal measures $35\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe diagonal is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{mm/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square opening, and $h$ be the length of its diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the relationship between $A$ and $h$ with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the area of the square is decreasing at a rate of $49{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-49.\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=35.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{35}\\cdot (-49) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{49}{35} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.4\\text{ mm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diagonal of the opening is decreasing at a rate of \n$1.4\\text{ mm/s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521531 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7218284d-fc0a-5fae-8251-9a209394cdcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7218284d-fc0a-5fae-8251-9a209394cdcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521019} :answer-field/id #uuid "d49bd1c4-d01f-5541-8a31-35843fff408d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98780b5d-6c50-5c2a-867a-a1e2a96de8f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-344494" :question/problem "A bright lantern $L$ is on the ground $18\\,\\text{ft}$ away from a wall and a $6\\,\\text{ft}$ tall actor is moving towards the wall at a rate of $3\\,\\text{ft/s}.$ To **one decimal place**, how fast is the height of the shadow on the wall decreasing when the actor is $8\\,\\text{ft}$ from the lantern?\n\nThe shadow decreases at a speed of approximately \n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/0a/0a47e801db651c027415a7943e5327bd378c1a08e533dc69673ab4304c8cc7b4.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the actor’s distance from the wall, while $y$ is the length of the shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the actor’s velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,\\text{ft/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the actor’s velocity is *negative* because it is moving *toward* the wall, and therefore the distance between it and the wall *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{(18-x)+x} & =\\frac{6}{18-x} \\\\ \\frac{y}{18} & =\\frac{6}{18-x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{108}{18-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{108}{18-x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{108}{(18-x)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=18-8=10\\,\\text{ft}$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,\\text{ft/s}$ into the above expression gives, to one decimal place\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{108}{{8}^{2}}\\cdot (-3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & ≈-5.1\\,\\text{ft/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shadow decreases at a speed of approximately  $5.1\\,\\text{ft/s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521538 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6081e871-d742-538d-8c66-3f9650b689ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{68.3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520983} {:answer/id #uuid "74962891-b2f9-59ae-a10f-151fcb5564ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{59.1}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521023} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0335af-9d97-523b-9116-2d597fe88f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{37.4}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521225} {:answer/id #uuid "d954596e-a926-5218-a588-5616b9c7a0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{88.1}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521249} {:answer/id #uuid "ee4c62da-63ab-593f-8dfe-728e7ea4a176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{46.3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6081e871-d742-538d-8c66-3f9650b689ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{68.3}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520983} :answer-field/id #uuid "183e2a0a-fbdb-53c6-baa6-7dc46eb5ab7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9231519-804c-5398-8af6-73a4e2d373bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-4743" :question/problem "![](images/61/61dc032461e469146f11eacac4f917e86d40b3078dc2d33ac89e8e0b31eefd48.png)\n\nRounded to one decimal place, $x≈$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given the lengths of the adjacent side and the hypotenuse, we can use cosine to find the angle measurement. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{10}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now calculate $x$ using inverse cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{10}{27} \\\\ \\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{10}{27}) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{10}{27}) \\\\ x & =(68.261\\,5…)^{∘} \\\\ x & ≈{68.3}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521543 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12c03d51-e827-5c7e-b631-35a43305197f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520804} {:answer/id #uuid "bb49dc6d-f812-549d-b816-15fa4e50e048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521182} {:answer/id #uuid "c2d3e687-f95d-56c9-991e-e43f37331b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521196} {:answer/id #uuid "df3c390d-6642-54ee-b5ac-caf9d0550e22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521263} {:answer/id #uuid "ee98e3cd-e735-5e78-950d-efe59ed9913a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb49dc6d-f812-549d-b816-15fa4e50e048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521182} :answer-field/id #uuid "191fb7c8-e3c8-5ad7-adce-9b609ebb5aad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9b48ed6-0697-5f61-bbb9-09dbd79829b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-158088" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{8}$ and $g(x)=kx.$ If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then we must have:\n\n$$\ng(f(x))=x\n$$\n\nTo find $g(f(x)),$ we write down the function $g$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =kx \\\\ g(f(x)) & =kf(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $f(x)=\\frac{x}{8}$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(f(x)) & =kf(x) \\\\  & =k(\\frac{x}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{8}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we also know that $g(f(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{8}x & =x \\\\ \\frac{k}{8}\\cancel{x} & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ \\frac{k}{8} & =1 \\\\ k & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521546 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "059a746a-7219-54b6-8c45-7f39d3a7b05a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520771} {:answer/id #uuid "2f35a4a7-d9a4-52b8-80c9-e437acc5e057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22.5\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520862} {:answer/id #uuid "6b377338-8877-5e4e-8347-0093dfa4516c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "47\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521007} {:answer/id #uuid "93b930d8-b345-570b-a7c7-dc0aff86eabe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521101} {:answer/id #uuid "ae39ea77-3301-5cbf-96b5-205a58c99d1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae39ea77-3301-5cbf-96b5-205a58c99d1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521153} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a87e9bd-e6be-51a6-8fce-13d2940093e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c95a9e41-829a-5344-af50-15a5606363d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-73369" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b4196d82bf27236e6c28651a01678c60c41bc851afbb7aad2e754578ba330e9.png)\n\nJacob is a carpenter. He wishes to reduce the size of a wooden square while maintaining its shape. The lengths of the diagonals of the square decrease at a rate of $2\\text{ cm}\\text{/h}.$ At what rate, in squared centimeters per hour, is the area of ​​the square decreasing when the length of the diagonal is $45\\,\\text{cm}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ decreases at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/h},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=45.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-2\\cdot 45=-90\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square is decreasing at a rate of $90\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db110e87-ecff-5df6-86c8-2144e4e980f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db110e87-ecff-5df6-86c8-2144e4e980f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521254} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c7ad603-536b-5d70-82a1-a923240f239d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d564be6e-5836-50c8-903c-6a70a3ceed94" :question/math-academy-id "q-344736" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bc7e248783ccf6b1eda95a1c2c80300e48d8cdb48f39ea07296ed3bf51bfaa29.png)\n\nA small, circular puddle evaporates on a floor. If the puddle’s area is decreasing at a rate of $32\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min},$ calculate the rate of change of its circumference at the moment when the radius is $8\\,\\text{cm}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe circumference of the puddle is decreasing at a rate of \n{{field-1}} $\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=8,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-32\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $r=8\\,\\text{cm},$ $C=2\\pi (8)=16\\pi \\text{ cm}.$ Hence, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-32\\pi $ and $C=16\\pi ,$ and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-32\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{16\\pi }{2\\pi } \\\\ -32\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 8 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the circumference is decreasing at a rate of $4\\pi \\text{ cm}\\text{/min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521556 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "217fd35f-043a-51ea-bb88-98a6243cbd0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520832} {:answer/id #uuid "36627598-707e-5fca-8a0d-d812713fd5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520881} {:answer/id #uuid "37962c12-aa55-552e-a5ef-02ce2e2500fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520885} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e8c220-fc4e-5e93-9ea0-f85a2d09e7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521133} {:answer/id #uuid "b460d5d1-82bb-564e-b699-500fb25f2615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37962c12-aa55-552e-a5ef-02ce2e2500fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520885} :answer-field/id #uuid "afac089e-3d46-5d44-b08d-1f8fc4927c12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da03fb8c-a7ca-5696-a1f6-3635e0d03dad" :question/math-academy-id "q-123550" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡{22}^{∘}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{90})$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡{94}^{∘}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{37\\pi }{90})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡{76}^{∘}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{19\\pi }{25})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${22}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{22}^{∘}=22\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{11\\pi }{90}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Converting ${94}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{94}^{∘}=94\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{47\\pi }{90}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{47\\pi }{90}\\ne \\frac{37\\pi }{90}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Converting ${76}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{76}^{∘}=76\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{19\\pi }{45}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{19\\pi }{45}\\ne \\frac{19\\pi }{25}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I only'."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521557 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14b2d8e5-9b5c-5216-aeff-851b31557845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+3)}^{2}+\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520812} {:answer/id #uuid "7c027f5d-f2be-54c0-ba10-816c2c3c2421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521046} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc48979-59bf-5cf0-ab38-814855efe50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521084} {:answer/id #uuid "d260d6cd-2693-5fac-b2ee-6098a1ef0f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(2x+3)}^{2}+17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521236} {:answer/id #uuid "f242b273-5aa7-5a27-95c1-4f47737537b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c027f5d-f2be-54c0-ba10-816c2c3c2421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521046} :answer-field/id #uuid "ece1c1a9-e6aa-5e0f-8f5d-a9fac0d03848" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "db65568c-c0a4-5de2-8240-a716b187636f" :question/math-academy-id "q-25250" :question/problem "Rewrite $-2{x}^{2}-6x+4$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2$. So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2({x}^{2}+3x-2)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3x-2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x-2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{17}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}+3x-2) & =-2({(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{17}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{17}{4}) \\\\  & =-2{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{17}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5be44bdb-8b94-5510-8afa-27e2682bad80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5be44bdb-8b94-5510-8afa-27e2682bad80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520971} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bc6154d-11f3-5894-9fac-15ff31065158" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dd3d0969-d794-5861-a620-0bc6a8d07956" :question/math-academy-id "q-344649" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cb048e7ac152ffbc740d611726871f395b4c6c4abcc324a4eed890e6116ec814.png)\n\nA manufacturing process gradually increases the size of a square opening by lengthening its diagonals at a rate of $1.6\\text{ mm}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the opening increasing when each diagonal measures $25\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\nThe area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ increases at a rate of $1.6\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=1.6.\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=25.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =1.6\\cdot 25=40{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square opening is increasing at a rate of \n$40{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521565 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eaa7604-4ed0-50fc-bc63-54add54bc183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}+{(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520793} {:answer/id #uuid "5111c904-bf94-52bf-b690-08709ab8e4c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76-{(y+9)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520949} {:answer/id #uuid "72adf21a-5264-5a28-871d-6fe472489501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521020} {:answer/id #uuid "76a0b804-79e8-5a39-9b18-44d9503bab79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{4}-{(y-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521026} {:answer/id #uuid "e13d59b2-3319-54ab-bbb7-cf745495f661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{4}-{(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e13d59b2-3319-54ab-bbb7-cf745495f661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{4}-{(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521268} :answer-field/id #uuid "2878baeb-9a33-5803-8177-df4c02bc48c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "edbc0b13-6a6e-5c66-aaa8-e675f3a4a51e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140409" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{y}^{2}-3y-5$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1$. So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}+3y+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+3y+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}y+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}$, writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}y+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}-3y-5 & =-({(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{4}-{(y+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fbc408b-c121-5287-963f-06a800c703d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520797} {:answer/id #uuid "132bb860-1537-5d50-8e8d-eab1cc552fe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520807} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee69202-d794-5dc7-b9e1-48471ca549b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520904} {:answer/id #uuid "8d9f692f-ebf1-58e1-8b6f-d036750bb7c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521085} {:answer/id #uuid "f63824fa-c71a-5d2f-8dd7-c1adb8d01387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f63824fa-c71a-5d2f-8dd7-c1adb8d01387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521304} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d98aaaa-2215-595e-9cfe-13f69f4453bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7892f8a-0a7a-5c55-9033-6382c732bb3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-123546" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡{110}^{∘}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{18})$\n\nII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡{140}^{∘}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{9})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡{170}^{∘}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{17\\pi }{18})$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${110}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{110}^{∘}=110\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{11\\pi }{18}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Converting ${140}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{140}^{∘}=140\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{7\\pi }{9}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Converting ${170}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{170}^{∘}=170\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{17\\pi }{18}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III'."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec3cb01-9709-5f4d-b512-edefe3f3ee1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{185}{4},- \\frac{55}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y - \\frac{5}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521677} {:answer/id #uuid "2f284a02-2fb2-5fa7-bd41-576dd3d0bba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{55}{2},\\frac{185}{4}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y - \\frac{5}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521689} {:answer/id #uuid "7d40b20a-3844-5ee1-965b-acec820bd31d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨- \\frac{185}{4},- \\frac{55}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y - \\frac{5}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521710} {:answer/id #uuid "7f241a59-debf-5de1-a40a-8220ae6d711f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{185}{4},- \\frac{55}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y + \\frac{5}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521712} {:answer/id #uuid "8a532d49-8d53-5a2f-a45c-6713f991d1b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{185}{4},- \\frac{55}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x - \\frac{3}{2},\\,y - \\frac{5}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ec3cb01-9709-5f4d-b512-edefe3f3ee1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{185}{4},- \\frac{55}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y - \\frac{5}{2}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521677} :answer-field/id #uuid "b665079c-c019-5428-b8de-1f2f603c7242" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "20ccc6f9-9078-5553-ae96-b36095f293ae" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=- 5 x^{2} + 5 x y^{2} + 2 y^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 5 x^{2} + 5 x y^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 5 x^{2} + 5 x y^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(- 10 x + 5 y^{2})\\,i+(10 x y + 4 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}) & =(- 10 (- \\frac{3}{2}) + 5 (\\frac{5}{2})^{2})\\,i+(10 (- \\frac{3}{2}) (\\frac{5}{2}) + 4 (\\frac{5}{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{185}{4})\\,i+(- \\frac{55}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{185}{4},- \\frac{55}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2})\\cdot ⟨x-(- \\frac{3}{2}),\\,y-(\\frac{5}{2})⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{185}{4},- \\frac{55}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y - \\frac{5}{2}⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21544ba6-ef06-5266-93a1-313a526067e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{85}{9},- \\frac{13}{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{5}{2},\\,y - \\frac{1}{3}⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521685} {:answer/id #uuid "2eb23044-bcc9-5962-9