 equations\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})-10,\\,y=1\\sin(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})+0,\\,\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\le t<\\frac{7\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-4\\sin(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n2\\cos(2t-\\frac{\\pi}{6})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{11\\pi}{6}, \\frac{7\\pi}{3}, \\frac{17\\pi}{6}, \\frac{10\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{11\\pi}{6}) & =4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{7\\pi}{3}) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{17\\pi}{6}) & =4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{10\\pi}{3}) & =-4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{11\\pi}{6}, \\frac{7\\pi}{3}, \\frac{17\\pi}{6}, \\frac{10\\pi}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d66d1357-e0d7-58c9-a48f-bd62562836f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d66d1357-e0d7-58c9-a48f-bd62562836f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523080} :answer-field/id #uuid "20ef8185-3b01-5cc2-a040-a6ce07a57c3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523027} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "396284fb-66e1-5043-b407-7357db7c21ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "396284fb-66e1-5043-b407-7357db7c21ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523036} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e4eda86-0324-54cf-b300-9a498fb8794e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da8bb941-6503-5014-b798-77c51b79d5e5" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+11,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the normal to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =-6\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) & =6\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13eb88b0-c538-59f5-a87f-9f0c62715e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{4}{3},\\,-\\frac{1}{3},\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523194} {:answer/id #uuid "24f1dbef-5fc8-59db-bec0-0a9c8a04f080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2 \\sqrt{26}}{13},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{26},\\,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{26}}{26}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523198} {:answer/id #uuid "8267fe08-c361-5516-bb08-f2287927df19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{11},\\,-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{11}}{11}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523214} {:answer/id #uuid "c20caa2a-1e3e-5e4e-8c04-1a085e20bfe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{26}}{13},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{26},\\,-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{26}}{26}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523224} {:answer/id #uuid "e89ef417-189c-5198-b034-c7b157620d77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{6}{13},\\,-\\frac{3}{26},\\,-\\frac{9}{26}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c20caa2a-1e3e-5e4e-8c04-1a085e20bfe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{26}}{13},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{26},\\,-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{26}}{26}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523224} :answer-field/id #uuid "13f6643a-75d9-50a2-a3fb-88100e3589a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b1292b8d-2b01-5533-896d-f2505b0485b1" :question/problem "In what unit direction does the function $f(x,y,z)=\\ln (x^{4}y^{1}z^{3})$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(3,3,3)$?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(3,3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (x^{4}y^{1}z^{3})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln (x^{4}y^{1}z^{3})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\ln (x^{4}y^{1}z^{3}))⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{1}{x^{4}y^{1}z^{3}}\\cdot 4x^{3}y^{1}z^{3},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{4}y^{1}z^{3}}\\cdot x^{4}y^{0}z^{3},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{4}y^{1}z^{3}}\\cdot 3x^{4}y^{1}z^{2}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{4}{x},\\,\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{3}{z}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(3,3,3) & =⟨\\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{3}{3}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding unit direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =-\\frac{∇f(3,3,3)}{‖∇f(3,3,3)‖} \\\\ & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{(\\frac{4}{3})^{2}+(\\frac{1}{3})^{2}+(1)^{2}}}⟨\\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,1⟩ \\\\ & =⟨-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{26}}{13},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{26},\\,-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{26}}{26}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the unit direction of $⟨-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{26}}{13},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{26},\\,-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{26}}{26}⟩.$"}]
